{"question": "Two weeks after undergoing an emergency cardiac catherization with stenting for unstable angina pectoris, a 61-year-old man has decreased urinary output and malaise. He has type 2 diabetes mellitus and osteoarthritis of the hips. Prior to admission, his medications were insulin and naproxen. He was also started on aspirin, clopidogrel, and metoprolol after the coronary intervention. His temperature is 38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F), pulse is 93/min, and blood pressure is 125/85 mm Hg. Examination shows mottled, reticulated purplish discoloration of the feet. Laboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin count 14 g/dL\nLeukocyte count 16,400/mm3\nSegmented neutrophils 56%\nEosinophils 11%\nLymphocytes 31%\nMonocytes 2%\nPlatelet count 260,000/mm3\nErythrocyte sedimentation rate 68 mm/h\nSerum\nUrea nitrogen 25 mg/dL\nCreatinine 4.2 mg/dL\nRenal biopsy shows intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms?\"", "options": {"A": "Renal papillary necrosis", "B": "Allergic interstitial nephritis", "C": "Cholesterol embolization", "D": "Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis", "E": "Polyarteritis nodosa"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Cardiology", "Nephrology", "Internal Medicine", "Hematology", "Pathology"], "option_domains": ["Nephrology", "Cardiology"], "question_analyses": {"Cardiology": "The patient's condition is highly concerning and involves multiple systems, with a primary focus on the cardiovascular and renal systems. The key aspects of the patient's presentation and laboratory findings suggest a diagnosis of **heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)** with associated thrombotic complications. ### Key Symptoms and Findings:\n1. **Decreased Urinary Output and Malaise**: These symptoms suggest renal dysfunction, which is supported by the elevated serum urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine levels. 2. **Mottled, Reticulated Purplish Discoloration of the Feet**: This 